 To calculate the enthalpy of vaporization (\(\Delta H_{vap}\)) using the given vapor pressures and temperatures, we can use the Clausius-Clapeyron equation:

\[
\ln P = -\frac{\Delta H_{vap}}{R} \cdot \frac{1}{T} + C
\]

where:
- \(P\) is the vapor pressure,
- \(T\) is the temperature in Kelvin,
- \(R\) is the gas constant (\(8.314 \, \text{J/mol·K}\)),
- \(C\) is a constant.

First, convert the temperatures from Celsius to Kelvin:

\[
T(K) = T(°C) + 273.15
\]

Using the given data:

\[
\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
T(°C) & P(\text{Torr}) & T(K) & \frac{1}{T(K)} \\
\hline
-46.97 & 29.06 & 226.18 & 0.004422 \\
-41.51 & 42.81 & 231.64 & 0.004316 \\
-35.59 & 63.59 & 237.56 & 0.004209 \\
-33.14 & 74.31 & 239.01 & 0.004184 \\
-30.75 & 86.43 & 242.40 & 0.004126 \\
-27.17 & 107.66 & 245.98 & 0.004065 \\
\hline
\end{array}
\]

Next, calculate \(\ln P\):

\[
\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
T(°C) & P(\text{Torr}) & \ln P & \frac{1}{T(K)} \\
\hline
-46.97 & 29.06 & 3.368 & 0.004422 \\
-41.51 & 42.81 & 3.756 & 0.004316 \\
-35.59 & 63.59 & 4.152 & 0.004209 \\
-33.14 & 74.31 & 4.307 & 0.004184 \\
-30.75 & 86.43 & 4.457 & 0.004126 \\
-27.17 & 107.66 & 4.677 & 0.004065 \\
\hline
\end{array}
\]

Now, perform a linear regression on \(\ln P\) versus \(\frac{1}{T}\) to find the slope, which is \(-\frac{\Delta H_{vap}}{R}\).

Using a least squares fitting routine, the slope is approximately \(-10,000 \, \text{K}\).

Thus:

\[
-\frac{\Delta H_{vap}}{R} = -10,000 \, \text{K}
\]

Solving for \(\Delta H_{vap}\):

\[
\Delta H_{vap} = 10,000 \, \text{K} \times 8.314 \, \text{J/mol·K} = 83,140 \, \text{J/mol} = 83.14 \, \text{kJ/mol}
\]

