 To find the magnitude of the velocity at launch and at landing, we need to analyze the projectile motion of the man.

### Step 1: Determine the initial velocity components
Given:
- Horizontal distance, \( D = 20.0 \, \text{m} \)
- Flight time, \( t = 2.50 \, \text{s} \)
- Launch angle, \( \theta_0 = 40.0^\circ \)

The horizontal component of the initial velocity \( v_{0x} \) can be found using the horizontal distance and time:
\[ v_{0x} = \frac{D}{t} = \frac{20.0 \, \text{m}}{2.50 \, \text{s}} = 8.00 \, \text{m/s} \]

The vertical component of the initial velocity \( v_{0y} \) can be found using the launch angle:
\[ v_{0y} = v_{0x} \tan(\theta_0) = 8.00 \, \text{m/s} \times \tan(40.0^\circ) \approx 8.00 \, \text{m/s} \times 0.8391 = 6.71 \, \text{m/s} \]

### Step 2: Calculate the magnitude of the initial velocity
The magnitude of the initial velocity \( v_0 \) is:
\[ v_0 = \sqrt{v_{0x}^2 + v_{0y}^2} = \sqrt{(8.00 \, \text{m/s})^2 + (6.71 \, \text{m/s})^2} \]
\[ v_0 = \sqrt{64.00 + 45.02} = \sqrt{109.02} \approx 10.44 \, \text{m/s} \]

### Step 3: Determine the velocity at landing
Since the man lands in the water pool, the vertical component of the velocity at landing will be the same as the initial vertical velocity but in the opposite direction (due to symmetry of projectile motion). The horizontal component remains the same.

Thus, the magnitude of the velocity at landing \( v_f \) is the same as the initial velocity:
\[ v_f = v_0 = 10.44 \, \text{m/s} \]

### Conclusion
The magnitude of the velocity at launch and at landing is \( 10.44 \, \text{m/s} \).

The correct option is:
B. 10.44

Answer: \boxed{B}
