   For a simply supported beam with a linearly varying distributed load, the maximum bending moment occurs at the midpoint of the beam. The load distribution is given by \( q(x) = \frac{x}{L} q_0 \).

   The total load \( W \) on the beam is:
   \[
   W = \int_0^L q(x) \, dx = \int_0^L \frac{x}{L} q_0 \, dx = \frac{q_0}{L} \int_0^L x \, dx = \frac{q_0}{L} \left[ \frac{x^2}{2} \right]_0^L = \frac{q_0}{L} \cdot \frac{L^2}{2} = \frac{q_0 L}{2}
   \]

   The maximum bending moment \( M_{\text{max}} \) at the midpoint \( x = \frac{L}{2} \) is:
   \[
   M_{\text{max}} = \frac{W \cdot \frac{L}{2}}{L} = \frac{\frac{q_0 L}{2} \cdot \frac{L}{2}}{L} = \frac{q_0 L^2}{4L} = \frac{q_0 L}{4}
