A function \( f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \) is surjective if for every \( y \in \mathbb{R} \), there exists an \( x \in \mathbb{R} \) such that \( f(x) = y \).

For the function \( f(x) = x^3 \):
- Given any real number \( y \), we can find an \( x \) such that \( x^3 = y \). Specifically, \( x = \sqrt[3]{y} \) is a real number for any real number \( y \).

Since for every \( y \in \mathbb{R} \), there exists an \( x \in \mathbb{R} \) such that \( f(x) = y \), the function \( f(x) = x^3 \) is surjective.

