\[
\left[ 100x^{0.01} \right]_1^b = 100b^{0.01} - 100 \cdot 1^{0.01} = 100b^{0.01} - 100
\]

Next, we take the limit as \(b\) approaches infinity:
\[
\lim_{b \to \infty} (100b^{0.01} - 100)
\]

Since \(b^{0.01}\) grows without bound as \(b\) approaches infinity, the term \(100b^{0.01}\) also grows without bound. Therefore, the limit is:
\[
\lim_{b \to \infty} (100b^{0.01} - 100) = \infty
\]

This means that the integral \(\int_1^{\infty} \frac{1}{x^{0.99}} \, dx\) diverges to infinity.

According to the graph provided, the area under the curve from 1 to infinity is infinite, which confirms our result.

