3. **Calculate the new height of the water:**
   - The volume of water remains the same, \(2 \mathrm{~m}^3\).
   - Let \(h\) be the new height of the water.
   - The volume of water in the new orientation is:
     \[
     V = \text{length} \times \text{width} \times \text{height} = 2 \mathrm{~m} \times 1 \mathrm{~m} \times h
     \]
   - Set the volume equal to the initial volume:
     \[
     2 \mathrm{~m}^3 = 2 \mathrm{~m} \times 1 \mathrm{~m} \times h
     \]
   - Solve for \(h\):
     \[
     h = \frac{2 \mathrm{~m}^3}{2 \mathrm{~m} \times 1 \mathrm{~m}} = 1 \mathrm{~m}
