 To solve this problem, let's denote the side length of the square as \( s \). The area of the square is then \( s^2 \).

When one corner of the square is folded into the middle of the square, it creates an irregular pentagon. The area of this pentagon is given to be a consecutive whole number to the area of the square. Let's denote the area of the pentagon as \( s^2 + 1 \) or \( s^2 - 1 \).

Since the area of the pentagon is created by folding one corner of the square into the middle, the area of the folded part is a right triangle with legs of length \( \frac{s}{2} \). The area of this triangle is:
\[
\text{Area of the triangle} = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{s}{2} \times \frac{s}{2} = \frac{s^2}{8}
\]

The area of the pentagon is the area of the square minus the area of the triangle:
\[
\text{Area of the pentagon} = s^2 - \frac{s^2}{8} = \frac{7s^2}{8}
\]

