 To solve this problem, we need to understand the path traced by point \( P \) as the square rolls along the line. The square has sides of length 10, and it rolls without slipping. This means that the distance the square travels along the line is equal to the arc length traced by point \( P \).

When the square rolls, point \( P \) traces a cycloid. A cycloid is the curve traced by a point on the rim of a circular wheel as the wheel rolls along a straight line. The length of one complete arc of a cycloid is given by the formula:

\[ L = 8r \]

where \( r \) is the radius of the circle. In this case, the square can be thought of as a circle with a radius equal to half the side length of the square, which is \( r = 5 \).

However, the problem states that the rolling stops when \( P \) first returns to the line. This means that the square has rolled a distance equal to its side length, which is 10. Since the square is rolling without slipping, the distance traveled by the square is equal to the arc length traced by point \( P \).

The length of the curve that \( P \) has traveled is the length of the arc of the cycloid that corresponds to the distance traveled by the square. Since the square has rolled a distance equal to its side length, the length of the curve that \( P \) has traveled is:

\[ L = 10 \pi \]

